.TH "DOCUMENT.ARIANOTIFY" "3JS" "March 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Document.ariaNotify \- Document: ariaNotify() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]ariaNotify()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Document\f[R]
interface queues a string of text to be announced by a screen reader.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
ariaNotify(announcement)
ariaNotify(announcement, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]announcement\f[R]
A string specifying the text to be announced.
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An options object containing the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]priority\f[R]
An enumerated value specifying the priority of the announcement.
Possible values are:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]normal\f[R]
The announcement has normal priority.
It will be spoken after any announcement that a screen reader is
currently making.
This is the default value.
.TP
\f[B]high\f[R]
The announcement has high priority.
It will be spoken immediately, interrupting any announcement that a
screen reader is currently making.
.RE
.RE
.SS Return value
None (\f[CR]undefined\f[R]).
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]\f[CB]ariaNotify()\f[B]\f[R] method can be used to
programmatically trigger a screen reader announcement.
This method provides similar functionality to ARIA live regions, with
some advantages:
.IP \(bu 2
Live regions can only make announcements following changes to the DOM,
whereas an \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcement can be made at any time.
.IP \(bu 2
Live region announcements involve reading out the updated content of the
changed DOM node, whereas \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcement content
can be defined independently of DOM content.
.PP
Developers often work around the limitations of live regions using
hidden DOM nodes with live regions set on them, which have their
contents updated with the content to be announced.
This is inefficient and error\-prone, and \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R]
provides a way to avoid such issues.
.PP
Some screen readers will read out multiple \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R]
announcements in order, but this cannot be guaranteed across all screen
readers and platforms.
Normally, only the most recent announcement is spoken.
It is more reliable to combine multiple announcements into one.
.PP
For example, the following calls:
.IP
.EX
document.ariaNotify(\(dqHello there.\(dq);
document.ariaNotify(\(dqThe time is now 8 o\(aqclock.\(dq);
.EE
.PP
would be better combined:
.IP
.EX
document.ariaNotify(\(dqHello there. The time is now 8 o\(aqclock.\(dq);
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcements do not require transient
activation; you should take care not to spam screen reader users with
too many notifications, as this could create a bad user experience.
.SS Announcement priorities
An \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcement with \f[CR]priority: high\f[R]
set is announced before an \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcement with
\f[CR]priority: normal\f[R] set.
.PP
\f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcements are roughly equivalent to ARIA
live region announcements as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] \f[CR]priority: high\f[R]:
\f[CR]aria\-live=\(dqassertive\(dq\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] \f[CR]priority: normal\f[R]:
\f[CR]aria\-live=\(dqpolite\(dq\f[R].
.PP
However, \f[CR]aria\-live\f[R] announcements will take priority over
\f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcements.
.SS Language selection
Screen readers choose an appropriate voice with which to read
\f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] announcements (in terms of accent,
pronunciation, etc.)
based on the language specified in the \f[CR]<html>\f[R] element\(cqs
\f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute, or the user agent\(cqs default language if no
\f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute is set.
.SS Permissions policy integration
Usage of \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] in a document or \f[CR]<iframe>\f[R]
can be controlled by an \f[CR]aria\-notify\f[R] Permission Policy.
.PP
Specifically, where a defined policy blocks usage, any announcements
created using \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] silently fail (they will not be
sent).
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] usage
This example includes a \f[CR]<button>\f[R] that fires a screen reader
announcement when clicked.
.IP
.EX
<button>Press</button>
.EE
.IP
.EX
document.querySelector(\(dqbutton\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () => {
  document.ariaNotify(\(dqHi there, I\(aqm Ed Winchester.\(dq);
});
.EE
.SS Result
The output is as follows:
.PP
Try activating a screen reader and then pressing the button.
You should hear \(lqHi there, I\(cqm Ed Winchester.\(rq spoken by the
screen reader.
.SS Accessible shopping list example
This example is a shopping list that allows you to add and remove items,
and keeps track of the total cost of all items.
When an item is added or removed, screen readers will read out an
announcement to say what item was added/removed, and what the updated
total is.
.SS HTML
Our HTML features a \f[CR]<form>\f[R] containing two \f[CR]<input>\f[R]
elements \(em one \f[CR]text\f[R] input to enter item names into, and
one \f[CR]number\f[R] input to enter prices into.
Both inputs are \f[CR]required\f[R], and the \f[CR]number\f[R] input has
a \f[CR]step\f[R] value of \f[CR]0.01\f[R] to stop non\-price values
(like large decimals) being entered.
.PP
Below the form we have an unordered list to render added items in, and a
\f[CR]<p>\f[R] element to display the total cost.
.IP
.EX
<h1><code>ariaNotify</code> demo: shopping list</h1>

<form>
  <div>
    <label for=\(dqitem\(dq>Enter item name</label>
    <input type=\(dqtext\(dq name=\(dqitem\(dq id=\(dqitem\(dq required />
  </div>
  <div>
    <label for=\(dqprice\(dq>Enter item price</label>
    <input type=\(dqnumber\(dq name=\(dqprice\(dq id=\(dqprice\(dq step=\(dq0.01\(dq required />
  </div>
  <div>
    <button>Submit</button>
  </div>
</form>

<hr />

<ul></ul>

<p>Total: £0.00</p>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
Our script starts with several constant definitions to store references
to the \f[CR]<form>\f[R], our two \f[CR]<input>\f[R] elements, and our
\f[CR]<ul>\f[R] and \f[CR]<p>\f[R] elements.
We also include a \f[CR]total\f[R] variable to store the total price of
all items.
.IP
.EX
const form = document.querySelector(\(dqform\(dq);
const item = document.querySelector(\(dqinput[type=\(aqtext\(aq]\(dq);
const price = document.querySelector(\(dqinput[type=\(aqnumber\(aq]\(dq);
const priceList = document.querySelector(\(dqul\(dq);
const totalOutput = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

let total = 0;
.EE
.PP
In our next code block, we define a function called
\f[CR]updateTotal()\f[R] that has one job \(em it updates the price
displayed in the \f[CR]<p>\f[R] element to equal the current value of
the \f[CR]total\f[R] variable:
.IP
.EX
function updateTotal() {
  totalOutput.textContent = \(gaTotal: £${Number(total).toFixed(2)}\(ga;
}
.EE
.PP
Next, we define a function called \f[CR]addItemToList()\f[R].
Inside the function body we first create an \f[CR]<li>\f[R] element to
store a newly\-added item.
We store the item name and price in \f[CR]data\-*\f[R] attributes on the
element, and make its text content equal to a string containing the item
and price.
We also create a \f[CR]<button>\f[R] element with text of \(lqRemove
<item\-name>\(rq, then append the list item to the unordered list, and
the button to the list item.
.PP
The second major part of the function body is a \f[CR]click\f[R] event
listener definition on the button.
When the button is clicked, we first grab a reference to the button\(cqs
parent node \(em the list item it is inside.
We then subtract the number contained in the list item\(cqs
\f[CR]data\-price\f[R] attribute from the \f[CR]total\f[R] variable,
call the \f[CR]updateTotal()\f[R] function to update the shown total
price, then call \f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] to announce the item that was
removed, and what the new total is.
Finally, we remove the list item from the DOM.
.IP
.EX
function addItemToList(item, price) {
  const listItem = document.createElement(\(dqli\(dq);
  listItem.setAttribute(\(dqdata\-item\(dq, item);
  listItem.setAttribute(\(dqdata\-price\(dq, price);
  listItem.textContent = \(ga${item}: £${Number(price).toFixed(2)}\(ga;
  const btn = document.createElement(\(dqbutton\(dq);
  btn.textContent = \(gaRemove ${item}\(ga;

  priceList.appendChild(listItem);
  listItem.appendChild(btn);

  btn.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, (e) => {
    const listItem = e.target.parentNode;
    total \-= Number(listItem.getAttribute(\(dqdata\-price\(dq));
    updateTotal();
    document.ariaNotify(
      \(ga${listItem.getAttribute(
        \(dqdata\-item\(dq,
      )} removed. Total is now £${total.toFixed(2)}.\(ga,
      {
        priority: \(dqhigh\(dq,
      },
    );
    listItem.remove();
  });
}
.EE
.PP
Our final code block add a \f[CR]submit\f[R] event listener to the
\f[CR]<form>\f[R].
Inside the handler function, we first call \f[CR]preventDefault()\f[R]
on the event object to stop the form submitting.
We then call \f[CR]addItemToList()\f[R] to display the new item and its
price in the list, add the price to the \f[CR]total\f[R] variable, call
the \f[CR]updateTotal()\f[R] to update the displayed total, then call
\f[CR]ariaNotify()\f[R] to announce the item that was added, and what
the new total is.
Finally, we clear out the current input field values ready for the next
item to be added.
.IP
.EX
form.addEventListener(\(dqsubmit\(dq, (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();

  addItemToList(item.value, price.value);
  total += Number(price.value);
  updateTotal();

  document.ariaNotify(
    \(gaItem ${item.value}, price £${
      price.value
    }, added to list. Total is now £${total.toFixed(2)}.\(ga,
    {
      priority: \(dqhigh\(dq,
    },
  );

  item.value = \(dq\(dq;
  price.value = \(dq\(dq;
});
.EE
.SS Result
The output is as follows:
.PP
Try activating a screen reader and then adding and removing some items.
You should hear them announced by the screen reader.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.ariaNotify()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
ARIA live regions
